color-theme: genetics

=== layout: title-slide

# Lecture 05D

## Probability Concepts

## Choose the rule. Check the context.

## Dr. Neil Voss

@notes

Flow: open with the hard pollen problem; expose the addition-rule trap; build the tools;
return to the pollen problem; finish with conditional inheritance. Aim for about 25 minutes,
including two short discussion pauses. These notes are cues, not a script.

=== layout: one-panel

# Learning objectives

@body

- Distinguish overlap from independence
- Choose addition or multiplication
- Predict offspring probabilities from gamete frequencies
- Interpret a conditional probability

@notes

Students should be able to justify a rule, not just recall its equation. The same three
examples will carry the whole lecture. Preview that the biology determines the probabilities.

=== layout: two-panels

# How do we solve this???

@left

## Plants: Aa &times; Aa

- Equal A and a production
- Pollen success: A:a = 1:2
- Ovules: unaffected

@right

## Predict

F&#x2082; ratio: AA : Aa : aa = ?

Still 1 : 2 : 1?

Which probabilities would you need?

@notes

Opening challenge, adapted from the original pollen screenshot. Success means surviving
and participating in fertilization. Assume random union of the successful pollen with eggs
and equal survival of the resulting offspring. Take guesses without solving it yet.
Promise to return once we have tools for both a route to an offspring and alternative routes.

=== layout: two-panels

# What exactly are we counting?

@left

## [OR = at least one]{color=blue}

P(B or C)

Either or both events

Union

@right

## [AND = both]{color=purple}

P(B and C)

Both events

Intersection

@notes

Use B and C for events so they are not confused with the A allele. An event is an outcome
or set of outcomes we care about. Ask what ordinary-language "or" might leave ambiguous;
our probability OR includes both. Neither keyword alone licenses a shortcut.

=== layout: section

# Addition

## Can the events overlap?

@notes

Start where adding really works. Then change the biological context while keeping the
individual probabilities the same. That is the trap worth remembering.

=== layout: two-panels

# One gamete: A&#x2081; OR A&#x2082;?

@left

## Parent: A&#x2081;A&#x2082;

| Gamete | Probability |
| --- | --- |
| A&#x2081; | &#x00BD; |
| A&#x2082; | &#x00BD; |

@right

One allele at this locus

P(A&#x2081; or A&#x2082;) = &#x00BD; + &#x00BD; = 1

[Mutually exclusive = cannot both occur]{color=red}

@notes

Ask whether this ONE normal haploid gamete can carry both alleles at this locus. No.
Assume ordinary equal segregation. The two alternatives exhaust the possibilities here.
Mutually exclusive is also called disjoint. Keep "one gamete" explicit.

=== layout: one-panel

# Rule card: addition

@body

## OR &rarr; add alternatives

[P(B or C) = P(B) + P(C)]{color=blue}

Condition: [mutually exclusive]{color=red}

Overlap: P(B and C) = 0

@notes

Point back to the single-gamete example. This is the special addition rule, and its
condition is part of the rule. Keep this card in mind for the next problem.

=== layout: two-panels

# One offspring: A&#x2081; OR A&#x2083;?

@left

## A&#x2081;A&#x2082; &times; A&#x2083;A&#x2084;

One gene, four alleles

Equal segregation

Random fertilization

@right

B = inherits A&#x2081;

C = inherits A&#x2083;

[P(B or C) = ?]{color=purple}

Either allele, including both

@notes

This is the original four-allele screenshot rewritten as native text, with no textbook
exercise number. We now ask about one diploid offspring, not one haploid gamete.
Let students identify the two events before choosing an equation.

=== layout: two-panels

# So... every offspring qualifies?

@left

P(B) = &#x00BD;

P(C) = &#x00BD;

&#x00BD; + &#x00BD; = [100% ???]{color=red}

@right

## Vote: P(A&#x2081; or A&#x2083;)?

- A. 100%
- B. 75%
- C. 50%
- D. 25%

@notes

Present the tempting answer as a claim to test, not an answer to memorize. Vote before
the square: B is correct. The arithmetic is fine; the untested condition is the problem.
Ask whether one offspring could inherit both. Do not repair the claim yet.

=== layout: two-panels

# Test the 100% claim

@left

|  | A&#x2083; | A&#x2084; |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A&#x2081; | ? | ? |
| A&#x2082; | ? | ? |

@right

## Discuss

- Fill the four offspring
- Mark A&#x2081; OR A&#x2083;
- Find a counterexample

Think: 30 sec; pair: 1 min

@notes

Pairs or Zoom chat: fill the square, then decide which cells qualify. Expected counterexample:
A2A4. Each cell has probability &#x00BC; under the stated assumptions. Ask for a reason before
advancing; this is the main addition-rule misconception checkpoint.

=== layout: two-panels

# Three outcomes qualify

@left

|  | A&#x2083; | A&#x2084; |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A&#x2081; | [A&#x2081;A&#x2083;: BOTH]{color=purple} | A&#x2081;A&#x2084;: YES |
| A&#x2082; | A&#x2082;A&#x2083;: YES | [A&#x2082;A&#x2084;: NO]{color=red} |

@right

P(A&#x2081; or A&#x2083;) = [&#x00BE;]{color=blue}

Not 100%

Where did we count twice?

@notes

Keep the same square orientation throughout this sequence. Read the YES/NO/BOTH labels,
not just the colors. The shared A1A3 cell is the clue for repairing the rule.

=== layout: two-panels

# One offspring, counted twice

@left

|  | A&#x2083; | A&#x2084; |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A&#x2081; | [B and C]{color=purple} | B only |
| A&#x2082; | C only | Neither |

@right

B: top row &rarr; &#x00BD;

C: left column &rarr; &#x00BD;

[Shared cell: subtract one &#x00BC;]{color=red}

@notes

Trace the top row and then the left column. The shared cell is visited twice.
We subtract one copy, not the whole outcome: inclusive OR must still include it once.

=== layout: one-panel

# Rule card: addition, with overlap

@body

## OR &rarr; add, then remove the duplicate

[P(B or C) = P(B) + P(C) &minus; P(B and C)]{color=blue}

Mutually exclusive? The last term is zero.

@notes

This general rule works whether events overlap or not. The earlier special rule is
the zero-overlap case. The square gives the equation a meaning before we name it.

=== layout: two-panels

# Callback: fix the 100% claim

@left

|  | A&#x2083; | A&#x2084; |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A&#x2081; | [Both: &#x00BC;]{color=purple} | B only: &#x00BC; |
| A&#x2082; | C only: &#x00BC; | Neither: &#x00BC; |

@right

## General addition rule

P(B or C) = &#x00BD; + &#x00BD; &minus; &#x00BC;

= [&#x00BE; = 75%]{color=blue}

Count each cell once

@notes

Connect the formula to the same square. An alternative check is 1 minus the probability
of the neither cell. Keep the focus on why the subtraction is necessary.

=== layout: two-panels

# Same probabilities, different context

@left

## ONE gamete

A&#x2081; OR A&#x2082;

Cannot carry both

&#x00BD; + &#x00BD; = [1]{color=blue}

@right

## ONE offspring

A&#x2081; OR A&#x2083;

Can inherit both

&#x00BD; + &#x00BD; &minus; &#x00BC; = [&#x00BE;]{color=blue}

@notes

Addition takeaway: name the event, identify the unit being sampled, and check overlap.
Same numbers do not guarantee the same answer. Now ask how to calculate an AND probability
when drawing every outcome becomes inconvenient.

=== layout: section

# Multiplication

## Does one event change the other probability?

@notes

Use the familiar square first. Then return to the hard pollen problem with the same
method but unequal gamete probabilities. Independence is a condition, not a synonym for 50/50.

=== layout: two-panels

# One offspring: A&#x2081; AND A&#x2083;

@left

|  | A&#x2083;: &#x00BD; | A&#x2084;: &#x00BD; |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A&#x2081;: &#x00BD; | [BOTH: &#x00BC;]{color=purple} | &#x00BC; |
| A&#x2082;: &#x00BD; | &#x00BC; | &#x00BC; |

@right

&#x00BD; of the offspring inherit A&#x2081;

Of those, &#x00BD; also inherit A&#x2083;

[&#x00BD; &times; &#x00BD; = &#x00BC;]{color=purple}

@notes

Ask students to point to the AND cell before reading the calculation. Random union
means the allele from one parent does not change the allele probabilities from the other.
Multiplication is taking a fraction of a fraction.

=== layout: one-panel

# Rule card: multiplication

@body

## AND &rarr; multiply along a route

[P(B and C) = P(B) &times; P(C)]{color=purple}

Condition: [independent events]{color=red}

Independent = learning one does not change P(other)

@notes

This is the special multiplication rule. Independent events may occur together;
our A1 and A3 events do. We will revisit the distinction from mutually exclusive.

=== layout: two-panels

# Back to our hard question

@left

## Plants: Aa &times; Aa

Pollen success: A:a = 1:2

Ovules: unaffected

F&#x2082; ratio: AA : Aa : aa = ?

@right

## Successful pollen

Vote: P(A) = ?

- A. &#x00BC;
- B. &#x2153;
- C. &#x00BD;
- D. &#x2154;

@notes

Repeat the opening problem before solving it. Vote: B is correct. Equal production does
not imply equal contribution to offspring. We need frequencies among successful pollen,
not all pollen produced. A is an unnormalized weight; C ignores selection; D is the a
frequency. Explain these distractors after the next slide, not before the vote.

=== layout: one-panel

# Successful pollen: first find the weights

@body

| Pollen allele | Produced | Relative success | Weight |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| A | &#x00BD; | &#x00BD; | [&#x00BC;]{color=purple} |
| a | &#x00BD; | 1 | [&#x00BD;]{color=purple} |

@notes

Read one row at a time. The success column is relative, not a measured absolute survival
rate. Multiply production by relative success. Ask why these weights do not sum to 1.

=== layout: two-panels

# Turn weights into probabilities

@left

## Total weight

&#x00BC; + &#x00BD; = &#x00BE;

A share = (&#x00BC;) / (&#x00BE;) = [&#x2153;]{color=purple}

a share = (&#x00BD;) / (&#x00BE;) = [&#x2154;]{color=purple}

@right

## Among successful pollen

A : a = 1 : 2

Shares sum to 1

@notes

Normalize by the total weight. The denominator is the successful-pollen pool. This answers
the vote: B, &#x2153;. A relative success of &#x00BD; does not mean a final allele frequency of &#x00BC;.

=== layout: two-panels

# One genotype, two gamete pools

@left

## Ovule: egg allele

| Allele | Probability |
| --- | --- |
| A | &#x00BD; |
| a | &#x00BD; |

@right

## Pollen: sperm allele

| Allele | Probability |
| --- | --- |
| A | [&#x2153;]{color=purple} |
| a | [&#x2154;]{color=purple} |

@notes

Flowering plants have egg cells inside ovules. Pollen produces and delivers sperm cells.
Egg frequencies stay equal; sperm frequencies reflect the successful pollen pool.
Assume random union of these gametes: independence does not require equal frequencies.

=== layout: two-panels

# Your turn: weight the square

@left

## Pollen: sperm allele

| Ovule | A: &#x2153; | a: &#x2154; |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A: &#x00BD; | AA: &#x2159; | Aa: ? |
| a: &#x00BD; | Aa: ? | aa: ? |

@right

## Predict

AA: (&#x00BD;) &times; (&#x2153;) = &#x2159;

Fill the other three cells

30 sec; compare answers

@notes

Callback to the multiplication card: multiply along a route. Give the AA cell as a
worked example, then pause. Expected remaining values: top right &#x2153;, bottom left &#x2159;,
bottom right &#x2153;. Ask whether four cells still means four equally likely outcomes.

=== layout: two-panels

# Four cells, unequal probabilities

@left

## Pollen: sperm allele

| Ovule | A: &#x2153; | a: &#x2154; |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A: &#x00BD; | AA: &#x2159; | [Aa: &#x2153;]{color=blue} |
| a: &#x00BD; | [Aa: &#x2159;]{color=blue} | aa: &#x2153; |

@right

## Check

&#x2159; + &#x2153; + &#x2159; + &#x2153; = 1

Two routes to Aa

Can ONE offspring take BOTH routes?

@notes

The cells total 1. The Aa cells differ in which parent supplied A. For one offspring,
those routes are mutually exclusive. This is the place where the special addition rule
really does apply, even though the resulting genotype is the same.

=== layout: two-panels

# Aa: multiply within, add between

@left

## Ovule A AND pollen a

(&#x00BD;) &times; (&#x2154;) = &#x2153;

OR

Ovule a AND pollen A

(&#x00BD;) &times; (&#x2153;) = &#x2159;

@right

## Mutually exclusive routes

P(Aa) = &#x2153; + &#x2159;

= [&#x00BD;]{color=blue}

@notes

Physically trace across a route for multiplication and then between routes for addition.
Same genotype, different parental origins. The condition for adding is about the events,
not about whether their genotype labels look alike.

=== layout: two-panels

# Our opening problem, solved

@left

| Type | P | Ratio |
| --- | --- | --- |
| AA | &#x2159; | 1 |
| Aa | &#x00BD; | 3 |
| aa | &#x2153; | 2 |

@right

## [AA : Aa : aa = 1 : 3 : 2]{color=purple}

A pollen contributes less

Two routes produce Aa

@notes

Return explicitly to the guesses on the opening slide. We did not need a new probability
rule: we needed the right gamete pool and the old rules in the right places. Ratios are
expected proportions, not a guarantee that every six offspring fit the ratio exactly.

=== layout: two-panels

# Callback: two rules, two jobs

@left

## [AND: within a route]{color=purple}

Ovule A AND pollen a

&#x00BD; &times; &#x2154; = &#x2153;

Condition: random union

@right

## [OR: between routes]{color=blue}

Two exclusive routes to Aa

&#x2153; + &#x2159; = &#x00BD;

Condition: no overlap

@notes

Section takeaway: multiply within a route, add distinct routes, and state the conditions.
What if learning one event happened changes the other probability? That leads to the
general multiplication rule, using a conditional probability.

=== layout: section

# Conditional probability

## What changes when we know more?

@notes

Shift from changing the successful gamete pool to changing the information available.
Use the original Huntington inheritance example as a simplified classroom model.

=== layout: two-panels

# A family inheritance question

@left

## Huntington disease model

H = dominant disease allele

Affected father: Hh

Mother: hh

@right

## Their young adult son

Genotype unknown; no symptoms yet

Child's other parent: hh

Who could inherit H?

@notes

Adapt the third textbook screenshot without its exercise number. In this simplified
late-onset model, an H carrier will eventually develop disease; absence of symptoms at
this young age does not distinguish Hh from hh. Stipulate Hh for the affected father,
hh for both unrelated parents, and no new mutation. These assumptions define the exercise.
The next two questions preserve its original parts: the son's risk, then his child's.

=== layout: stacked-panels

# First: the son's probability?

@top

P(son inherits H) = ?

No symptoms yet: useful evidence?

@bottom

| Mother's egg | Father's sperm: H | Father's sperm: h |
| --- | --- | --- |
| h | ? | ? |

@notes

Pause for an answer before filling the two possible outcomes. The hh mother contributes
h to every child. Under our stated age assumption, lack of current symptoms does not
change the inheritance probability. Avoid treating symptom absence as a genotype test.

=== layout: stacked-panels

# The son: two possible genotypes

@top

P(son inherits H) = [&#x00BD;]{color=purple}

Same eventual disease risk, under our model

@bottom

| Mother's egg | Father's sperm: H | Father's sperm: h |
| --- | --- | --- |
| h: 1 | [Hh: &#x00BD;]{color=purple} | hh: &#x00BD; |

@notes

This answers the first part of the source question. Hh and hh are the only possibilities.
Keep inheritance and eventual symptoms distinct concepts, even though our simplified
model gives them the same probability. Now follow the allele for another generation.

=== layout: two-panels

# Next: his child's probability?

@left

## Son's genotype: unknown

Hh with probability &#x00BD;

hh with probability &#x00BD;

Child's other parent: hh

@right

## Vote: P(child inherits H)?

- A. 0
- B. &#x00BC;
- C. &#x00BD;
- D. 1

@notes

Vote: B is correct. The child has a &#x00BD; transmission probability only IF the son has H.
An hh son cannot transmit H in this model. Ask whether &#x00BD; applies on both branches.
Distinguish not knowing the son's genotype from knowing he is Hh.

=== layout: one-panel

# Follow both possible routes

@body

| Son's genotype | Probability | P(child H given genotype) | Route probability |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Hh | &#x00BD; | &#x00BD; | [&#x00BC;]{color=purple} |
| hh | &#x00BD; | 0 | 0 |
| Either | 1 |  | [Total: &#x00BC;]{color=purple} |

@notes

Define S as the son has H and C as his child has H. Multiply within each route:
P(S and C) = (&#x00BD;)(&#x00BD;) = &#x00BC;. Add the mutually exclusive routes: P(C) = &#x00BC; + 0 = &#x00BC;.
This answers the second part. P(C given S) is &#x00BD;, whereas P(C) is &#x00BC;.
The table uses native editable text; there is no screenshot to decode.

=== layout: one-panel

# Rule card: general multiplication

@body

S = son has H; C = child has H

[P(S and C) = P(S) &times; P(C given S)]{color=purple}

P(C given S) = P(C | S)

Conditional = restricted to a known event

Independent? P(C given S) = P(C)

@notes

The vertical bar means "given," not OR or division. Here S is the son has H and C is
the child has H. These events are dependent: knowing S changes C from &#x00BC; to &#x00BD;.
The general rule works for independent events too, reducing to the earlier rule card.

=== layout: two-panels

# Can we reverse "given"?

@left

## Parent &rarr; child

P(child H given son H) = &#x00BD;

Known: the son has H

@right

## Vote: child &rarr; parent

P(son H given child H) = ?

- A. 0
- B. &#x00BC;
- C. &#x00BD;
- D. 1

@notes

Vote: D is correct. Restate that the child's other parent is hh and we assume no new mutation.
Where could the child's H have come from? This is a reversal of information, not a
reversal of biological inheritance. Expected answer: 1, not &#x00BD;.

=== layout: two-panels

# The evidence identifies the route

@left

| Possible source of H | Fits the model? |
| --- | --- |
| The son | YES |
| The other parent: hh | NO |
| New mutation | Excluded |

@right

P(son H given child H) = [1]{color=purple}

P(child H given son H) = &#x00BD;

[Direction matters]{color=red}

@notes

Correct the reversed conditional in the old deck: with these assumptions, a child who
has inherited H establishes that the son has H. This does not make the unconditional
probability for the child 100%; that remains &#x00BC; before observing the child's genotype.

=== layout: two-panels

# Bayes: keep only the known group

@left

## We know: the child has H

P(son H and child H) = &#x00BC;

P(child H) = &#x00BC;

P(son H given child H) = [1]{color=purple}

@right

## Divide by P(C)

P(S | C) = P(S and C) / P(C)

= [P(C | S) &times; P(S) / P(C)]{color=purple}

Here: (&#x00BD; &times; &#x00BD;) / (&#x00BC;)

@notes

S means the son has H; C means the child has H. The denominator must be nonzero.
All of the probability mass in the child-H group belongs to the son-H route, so the
conditional fraction is 1. Name Bayes' rule after this reasoning. No extra formula image
or memorization exercise is needed. The expanded denominator was already built in the route table.

=== layout: two-panels

# Independent is not mutually exclusive

@left

## One gamete: A&#x2081; / A&#x2082;

Cannot carry both

Mutually exclusive: YES

Independent: NO

Knowing A&#x2081; rules out A&#x2082;

@right

## One offspring: A&#x2081; / A&#x2083;

Can inherit both

Mutually exclusive: NO

Independent: YES

Knowing A&#x2081; leaves P(A&#x2083;) = &#x00BD;

@notes

Return to the four-allele cross and its random-fertilization assumption. Compare the
same two questions: can both happen, and does knowing one change the other probability?
For the gamete, P(A2) changes from &#x00BD; to zero after learning A1. For the offspring,
P(A3) stays &#x00BD; after learning A1. Ask students to explain one side in their own words.

=== layout: one-panel

# Exit check: name the condition

@body

| Familiar problem | Which rule, and why? |
| --- | --- |
| A&#x2081; OR A&#x2083; in one offspring | ? |
| Two gamete routes to Aa | ? |
| Child H, given son H | ? |

@notes

Give 30 seconds to name a rule and its condition, not just a number. Ask students to
choose one row to explain aloud or in chat. This checks transfer between our examples
without introducing another story at the end of the lecture.

=== layout: one-panel

# The condition chooses the rule

@body

| Problem | Reasoning | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A&#x2081; OR A&#x2083; | Add; subtract overlap | &#x00BE; |
| Two routes to Aa | Multiply within; add between | &#x00BD; |
| Child inherits H | Given an Hh parent | &#x00BD; |

@notes

For row two, we mean the unequal-pollen example: &#x2153; plus &#x2159;. For row three, compare
the conditional &#x00BD; with the unconditional child probability of &#x00BC;. If students give
only an operator, ask what makes that operator valid here.

=== layout: one-panel

# Summary: context before calculation

@body

- Define the event and the population
- OR: add; subtract shared outcomes
- AND: multiply; check independence
- Given: restrict to the known group
- Check totals against the biology

@notes

Close the loop: the opening pollen puzzle needed successful-pollen frequencies, multiplication
within routes, and addition between routes. The four-allele trap exposed overlap; the family
example exposed direction in conditional probabilities. Ask: which condition will you check
before using each rule? Leave this summary visible as the deck ends.
